This research, “Architecting Resilience: A Practical Evaluation of IDS and UTM in a Windows Enterprise Defence-in-Depth,” evaluated the practical effectiveness of combining endpoint firewall protection, intrusion detection and unified threat management within a Windows enterprise security architecture.
The practical evaluation demonstrated that each security control provided a distinct and complementary defensive capability. Windows Defender Firewall successfully enforced endpoint-level access-control policies and restricted unauthorised traffic. Snort IDS successfully detected predefined suspicious network activity and generated corresponding security alerts. pfSense UTM successfully enforced gateway-level traffic filtering, including the blocking of TCP/443 traffic originating from the Kali Linux testing host.
The results demonstrate that defence-in-depth is not achieved simply by deploying multiple security technologies, but by appropriately positioning and configuring complementary controls at different points within the network architecture. The experiments also demonstrated that the effectiveness of each control is dependent on the traffic path being evaluated. In particular, gateway controls such as pfSense can only enforce policies on traffic that traverses the gateway, highlighting the importance of appropriate network architecture and segmentation.
Overall, the findings support the central proposition of the research that a layered approach combining endpoint prevention, network detection and gateway enforcement provides broader security coverage than reliance on a single security mechanism. The successful results from the practical scenarios provide an empirical foundation for the proposed Windows enterprise defence-in-depth architecture.
However, the research was conducted within a controlled virtual laboratory and was primarily limited to a local-subnet topology. These limitations provide opportunities for further investigation rather than diminishing the findings of the current study.
Future research, “Architecting Resilience: A Practical Evaluation of IDS and UTM in a Windows Enterprise Defence-in-Depth II,” will build upon this work by extending the architecture to multiple network segments within a physical laboratory environment. This will enable further evaluation of inter-subnet traffic, east-west security, IDS visibility, UTM enforcement, scalability and the effectiveness of security-control placement under more representative enterprise conditions.
Thus, this research provides a practical foundation for continued investigation into how layered security architecture can improve the resilience of Windows enterprise environments against evolving network threats.
